{
  "schema_version": 31,
  "name": "the menagerie",
  "description": "Chess on a board one rank taller, with a menagerie living in the open ground between the armies, and every creature in it changes the pieces round it. Two gorgons freeze the pieces on the eight squares round them: a frozen piece cannot move and threatens nothing. Two sirens disarm the pieces round them, which may still move but cannot capture or threaten. Two dryads shield the pieces round them, which nothing may capture. The creatures take nothing themselves. After every turn each walks one step along its own short stretch of rank, turns at the end and walks back, and turns round early when anything stands in its way; a player may take one, and it comes back to the start of its walk eight turns later. The duck is the herder. Nobody owns it and nothing can take it; it starts on the very centre of the board, and every turn ends with the player who has just moved walking it one king's step onto an empty square. Put in a creature's path, the duck turns it back. So each turn asks two questions: what to move, and where to push the herd, whether a gorgon toward the other side's defenders, a siren onto a piece about to strike, or a dryad beside a piece of your own that is under threat, knowing that the opponent will push back on the next turn. The game is won by taking the king, not by mate. A player left with no move at all, for instance with every piece frozen, is stalemated, and the game is drawn.",
